<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 28px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 18px; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> All Week Long!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's the Weekend.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write their own name and other very familiar words without assistance. Interactive: Draw pictures related to a simple sentence they have written.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write their name and days of the week without assistance (e.g., "Yamileth" and "Tuesday"). Can write simple greetings on cards (e.g., "Happy Monday!"). Can draw pictures for sentences they have written (e.g., a greeting card for the sentence "Happy Monday!").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads the class in singing a "Days of the Week" song, using flashcards for each day as they are mentioned. Students point to the corresponding flashcard. The teacher then asks, "What day is today?" and writes the answer on the board.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models writing their own name on the board, sounding out the letters. Then, the teacher writes "Today is [current day, e.g., Monday]" on the board, pointing to each word as it is written. They show how to form each letter clearly. Pronunciation of /w/ in "weekend" and /d/ in "day" is reinforced by exaggerating the sounds while pointing to the words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a number or a word?" (pointing to a written day).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you show me your name with your fingers?" (Students trace their name in the air).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What day is today? Say it with me." (Choral repetition).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large flashcards displaying simple sentences related to days of the week, e.g., "Monday is busy." "Saturday is exciting!" "Sunday is relaxing." Each flashcard includes a simple, clear picture (e.g., a person working for 'busy', a smiling child for 'exciting', a child sleeping for 'relaxing').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand and recognize very simple written sentences using the Noun + verb + noun structure (e.g., "Today is Monday.") and Present simple tense (e.g., "It is the weekend.") related to days and activities, identifying the sounds of letters within familiar words (phonemic awareness).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a flashcard (e.g., "Monday is busy.") and asks, "What day is this?" Students respond chorally or individually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads a sentence aloud (e.g., "Today is Saturday. It is relaxing.") and asks students to point to the flashcard that matches the sentence and picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'Friday is relaxing' True or False?" (based on a visual flashcard that shows Friday as busy).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing their name, the days of the week, and simple Noun + verb + noun sentences (e.g., "Today is Monday."). Using vocabulary: Monday, Tuesday, weekend, day, week, today, exciting, busy, relaxing. Reinforcing phonemic awareness by focusing on the letters in these words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Tracing):</b> Students receive a worksheet with dotted lines of their name and the names of the days of the week (Monday, Tuesday, Saturday, Sunday). They carefully trace over these words. The teacher circulates, providing individual guidance on letter formation and reminding students of the sound for each letter (phonemic awareness).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Copy & Complete):</b> The teacher writes "Today is _______." and "Happy _______!" on the board. Students choose a day from their traced list (or a flashcard) to copy and complete the sentences on their worksheet. They also write their own name at the top of the paper. For example, "Today is Monday." or "Happy Sunday!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student shows their completed sentences to their partner. The partner points to the written day (e.g., "Monday") and tries to say it. If the partner struggles, the writer helps them. This reinforces recognition and pronunciation (e.g., /w/ in 'weekend', /d/ in 'day').</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Students will create a "Daily Greeting Card." Each student receives a small piece of cardstock. They will choose one day of the week or "weekend" to write a greeting for.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students write a simple greeting on their card, such as "Happy Monday!" or "It is the weekend!" or "Sunday is relaxing." (using Noun + verb + noun and Present simple).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Below the greeting, they write their own name.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They then draw a picture that illustrates their greeting or the feeling of the day. For "Happy Monday!", they might draw a happy face or children playing. For "It is the weekend!", they might draw a relaxing scene.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages the use of adjectives like "exciting," "busy," or "quiet" if students are able.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> This task focuses on productive writing, allowing students to independently produce familiar words and short sentences, demonstrating their ability to write their name and simple greetings while reinforcing the theme "It's the Weekend" and the scenario "All Week Long!" through a personal artifact.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the production task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students individually present their "Daily Greeting Card" to the teacher or to a small group. They hold up their card, showing their writing and drawing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can write their own name without assistance.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can write at least one target day of the week or "weekend" (e.g., Monday, Saturday).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can write a simple greeting (e.g., "Happy Monday!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Drawing is clearly related to the written sentence.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates basic phonemic awareness by attempting to write familiar words.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a simple paper-based exit ticket by circling "Yes" or "No":
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write my name: Yes / No"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write 'Monday': Yes / No"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompts:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did I write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or hard to write 'Happy Monday!'? Why?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the majority of students successfully write their names and at least one target day/greeting?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the tracing and copying activities effective in building their writing confidence and letter formation?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments are needed for sentence complexity or drawing tasks for students who struggled or excelled? (e.g., provide more tracing practice, introduce another simple adjective like 'fun').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did students demonstrate phonemic awareness when writing familiar words?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will use their written "Daily Greeting Cards" to create a collaborative "Classroom Day Calendar" or a "Weekend Activities" display, consolidating their learning from this writing lesson with other skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>